Classic (Lua): a port of the classic computer player (src/Empire Earth/AI Thread/, the C++ classic of this
repository, the "classic" engine of the lobby) to the per-unit Lua interface (docs/AI_LUA_UNITS.md). Its manifest
entry is `classic_lua` (ai/ais.json). docs/AI_RESEARCH.md Â§8.39 has the benches.

What is ported: the decisions (what to train, build and research and when; the gatherer split; the epoch timer; the
building counts; the resource centres' claims; the attack timer, the targets and the groups' tasks), with the C++
classic's thresholds and timings read from the same data. What stays C++ (ENGINE): pathfinding, movement, formations,
combat, the units' own goals (the gather/return loop, auto-acquire), the production queues, and the cheats that act
in the engine. The C++ classic is the reference, including this repository's changes to it (the `balanced`
personality's rules behind aipersonalities.yaml switches); the `classic` personality switches them off.

Personality and difficulty: ai.tuning() (a query added for this port, docs/AI_LUA_UNITS.md Â§11) returns what the C++
classic reads for the seat: the personality as EECPPersonality picks it (aipersonalities.yaml `assign`, the TEST ONLY
EE_AI_PERSONALITY override, the EE_AI_EXP experiment switches folded in), the difficulty, the cheats on
(gameoptions.yaml ai_cheats, EE_AI_CHEATS), the map's kind and every dbcpbehavior value EEComputerPlayer::Init copies.
Floats come as thousandths (keys ending _k). tune.lua holds the classic's accessors that pick a value by difficulty.

The cheats on a Lua seat:
  cost     ENGINE: EEComputerPlayer::ScaleCost (mIsCheating from EECPCheatOn in Init) scales what the seat pays and
           what ai.class / ai.techs / the view report; the same as for a classic seat.
  reveal   ENGINE: EEComputerPlayer::Update's first tick explores the map (before the engine branch).
  extra    ENGINE: the same first tick adds the gold and iron.
  popband  ENGINE: WorkOutPopulation runs in EECPNextUpdate as in the classic tick; view.population_cap is the cap.
  epochs   ENGINE for the payment (EEComputerPlayer::Pay skips an epoch's cost; EECPNextCanAfford answers yes for an
           epoch); the port's own CanAfford and the needs ledger skip the epoch's cost as the classic's do.
  catchup  PORTED (civ.lua): the epoch timer's scale ahead of / behind the humans, the 60 s hold when ahead, and
           CatchUpEpoch's pull when a human reaches an epoch (the engine calls the C++ manager's; the port watches the
           humans' epochs in the view). DEVIATION: the humans' epochs are those the view's enemies carry.
  retire   ENGINE: EnterEpoch -> Personnel::RetireObsoleteUnits runs for any computer seat.

The engine's reflexes for this seat: the militia and the danger zones (EECPRMMilitia / EECPRMNoteDanger) skip the
units a script holds, and every citizen of this seat is held, so militia.lua ports them (ai.reflex{militia = false,
danger = false}); the naval flight stays the engine's (it does not skip held units).

